BT /F2 14 Tf 0.01 0.43 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 50 713.0 Tm (ALLOCATION AND DIRECTORIES) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 679.0 Tm (Block groups are intended to keep related file data, inodes and directories close on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 662.0 Tm (rotating media. Bitmaps represent allocated blocks and inodes, avoiding a single FAT-like) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 645.0 Tm (allocation chain. A file's inode points to data blocks or indirect blocks, and free-space) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 628.0 Tm (search uses group-local information. The physical placement strategy is a policy choice,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 611.0 Tm (not an intrinsic guarantee of contiguity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 582.0 Tm (Directories store inode-number and name pairs as variable-length records that must be) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 565.0 Tm (bounded by record lengths and block sizes. Hard links are multiple directory names) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 548.0 Tm (referencing one inode; their link counts matter for recovery. Symbolic links can store) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 531.0 Tm (small targets inside an inode. Unix permission bits and file types live in inode metadata,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.15 Tf 0.08 0.15 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 50 514.0 Tm (unlike simple DOS file attributes.) Tj ET
